*.log
.DS_Store
config.local.yml

# What compose.yml mounts into /config: a real config, a real passwd file, and the
# secret files beside them. None of it belongs in a repository.
#
# The leading slash is load-bearing, not tidiness. A gitignore pattern containing no
# slash of its own matches at *every* level, so `config/` also matched
# internal/config/ — §3's configuration package — and it was never committed. The
# working tree looked clean the whole time, because an ignored file is not an
# untracked one, and CI failed on `go mod tidy` trying to resolve
# github.com/nrosier/labview/internal/config as an external module. Anchor anything
# whose name a Go package could plausibly share.
/config/

# Compiled binaries. `go build ./cmd/labview` writes ./labview into this directory,
# whose name collides with the directory itself — hence the leading slash.
/labview
/labview.exe

# Nothing is ignored for the frontend, and that is the whole of §2.2's build story:
# the bundle under internal/webui/dist is hand-authored and committed, with no
# bundler, no dependency tree and no lockfile to reproduce — which is the strongest
# form of "reproducible offline from a committed lockfile" available, since there is
# nothing left to resolve. `go:embed` reads it at compile time, so `go build` and
# `go test` work in a checkout with no Node installed, and `go build` is the whole
# build (see the Dockerfile, which has no frontend stage for the same reason).
#
# assets/contract.js is generated from internal/webui/contract.go and committed
# beside the hand-written modules; a test fails when the committed bytes are stale.
# Regenerate with:
#
#     go test ./internal/webui -run TestContractAsset -update
